AD  - University of Information Technology, Faculty of Information Science and Engineering, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Viet Nam
AB  - Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) learn partial differential equation (PDE) solutions by minimizing physic residuals over collocation points. However, in practice, the learned residual field can become locally inconsistent, in which neighboring points may exhibit large residual gaps, producing a highly irregular error landscape that is jagged and misaligned with the trend that the true PDE solution typically implies. This mismatch is not only a local artifact; it can propagate, causing the model to follow the wrong global shape, even when the average residual decreases. We propose a Graph-based Adaptive Curriculum via Roughness Estimation for PINNs (GracePINN), which is a training framework that explicitly targets neighborhood inconsistency. GracePINN builds a spatio-temporal graph over collocation points and estimates the sample difficulty from the residual contrast within local neighborhoods, that is, how much a point’s residual deviates from nearby residuals. This roughness-aware difficulty is then converted into per-sample weights for the PDE term, encouraging the network to “smooth” the learned residual landscape so that its local trend better matches the underlying physics. Experiments on the benchmark demonstrate that GracePINN consistently improves the solution accuracy and stability across a range of PDEs with complex geometries and localized features while maintaining competitive performance in simpler settings. These results confirm that enforcing neighborhood-level residual consistency is an effective and general strategy for improving the PINN training dynamics. © 2013 IEEE.
