AD  - Shenzhen Polytechnic University, Shenzhen, China
AB  - Accurate forecasting of multivariate sensor signals from wind turbine (WT) components is paramount for proactive maintenance and ensuring the structural integrity of modern wind energy systems. However, industrial supervisory control and data acquisition time series often exhibit strong non-stationarity, complex spatial correlations among heterogeneous sensors, and significant measurement redundancy, which pose severe challenges to conventional forecasting models. To address these issues, this paper proposes the FS-iTransformer, a feature enhanced inverted Transformer framework, where FS denotes feature-enhanced and spatial-aware. Specifically, a feature-enhanced convolutional attention module is integrated to adaptively recalibrate sensor channels through multi-scale kernels, effectively capturing the coupled dynamics between components with high parameter efficiency. Furthermore, a spatially-aware convolution module is strategically incorporated to suppress feature redundancy by reconstructing the spatial and channel components of the sensory data. Experimental results on two authentic WT datasets demonstrate that FS-iTransformer achieves average reductions of 9.5% in mean absolute error and 10.9% in mean squared error compared to state-of-the-art baselines. These findings validate that the synergy of feature-enhanced embeddings and spatial-channel reconstruction provides a superior solution for high-precision industrial monitoring in complex sensing environments. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.
