UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047772669?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Energy and Transportation Engineering, Inner Mongolia Agricultural University, 306 Zhaowuda Road, Inner Mongolia, Hohhot, 010018, China
AB  - Efficiency evaluation in urban public transport systems is often hindered by “black-box” methodologies that fail to directly inform structural network redesign under fiscal constraints. To address this, this study proposes a hybrid decision-support framework to empirically evaluate two competing service philosophies: direct-service versus transfer-based (accessible) network designs. The framework integrates a multi-model machine learning (ML) classification strategy with a Färe-style two-stage network Data Envelopment Analysis (DEA). First, to overcome small-sample overfitting, a consensus-based ML approach—incorporating Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, SVM, and MLP—is employed to classify the intrinsic topologies of bus networks across 40 global cities based on nine structural and operational indicators. Under strict out-of-sample validation (LOOCV), SVM demonstrated superior generalization, ensuring highly robust network identification. Second, the network DEA explicitly decomposes system performance into a sequential production process: an input-oriented infrastructure-to-capability stage and an output-oriented capability-to-service stage. The empirical results, further validated by a non-oriented Slack-Based Measure (SBM) robustness check, reveal that transfer-based networks achieve approximately 69% higher overall efficiency than direct-service designs. This superiority is fundamentally driven by Stage 1 performance; by concentrating resources into high-frequency corridors and coordinated transfer nodes, accessible networks effectively eliminate physical redundancy and nearly double infrastructure utilization efficiency. The findings provide transit agencies with an actionable diagnostic tool, demonstrating that improving system-wide performance requires transitioning from incremental point-to-point route proliferation toward accessibility-oriented structural restructuring. © 2026 The Author(s)
