AB  - Accurate prediction of rockburst intensity levels is critical for the safety of deep underground engineering construction. This paper develops a Fuzzy-Regularized Graph Convolutional Network (FR-GCN) for long-term rockburst intensity assessment at the design or pre-construction stage. First, by integrating feature similarity and the correlation of engineering criteria, a multi-branch GCN structure was employed to effectively capture the physical characteristics of nodes, local neighborhood relationships, and topology patterns dominated by engineering criteria. To overcome the information loss and instability caused by hard threshold division, a fuzzy regularization mechanism was introduced, mapping discrete criterion features to continuous membership degree vectors and enhancing prediction consistency through a smoothing regularization term. Subsequently, the proposed model was validated using 197 real underground engineering cases collected globally. The five-fold cross-validation yields an average accuracy of 94.42%, demonstrating the superior predictive performance of the FR-GCN model. Further comparisons with traditional empirical criteria and existing machine learning models confirm the model's accuracy and stability. Additionally, preliminary engineering verification using 15 samples from two gold mines achieved 100% classification accuracy, providing preliminary evidence of the model's engineering application potential. Finally, an ablation study was conducted to analyze the contribution of each component to the model performance, indicating that the fuzzy regularization mechanism contributes to improving the model's stability and accuracy. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
