AD  - Key Laboratory of Coast Civil Structure Safety of Ministry of Education, Tianjin University, Tianjin, China
AB  - This paper investigates the seismic behavior of a fully bolted assembled inter-module joint for an innovative multi-story staggered modular steel structure. Unlike conventional bolted joint systems in modules aligned vertically, which suffer from complex “multi-beam and multi-column” intersections leading to material redundancy and complicated stress paths. Through quasi-static tests on three full-scale joint specimens, the effects of corner fitting thickness and stiffening plate type were examined. The primary failure modes involved local buckling and tearing of the corner fittings. The results showed that using a triquetrous plate as a stiffener increased the joint's maximum moment by 53.4 %, and increasing the corner fitting thickness from 5 mm to 10 mm boosted it by 70.6 %. A validated finite element model further revealed that increasing the corner fitting thickness to 15 mm improved the initial stiffness and maximum moment by 21 % and 11 %, respectively. Finally, a three-fold line restoring force model was established and validated with test results, providing an effective analytical tool for the seismic design of this novel staggered structural system. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
