TI  - Dynamic graph learning for forecasting coupled winds in twin tower structures during typhoons
PY  - 2026
T2  - Engineering Structures
VL  - 367
C7  - 123562
DO  - 10.1016/j.engstruct.2026.123562
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047885734?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong
AD  - National Rail Transit Electrification and Automation Engineering Technology Research Center (Hong Kong Branch), Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong
AD  - PolyU-Hangzhou Technology and Innovation Research Institute, Hangzhou, 310015, China
AD  - China State Construction Engineering (Hong Kong) Ltd., 999077, Hong Kong
AD  - China State Construction International Medical Industry Development Co., Ltd., 999077, Hong Kong
AB  - During typhoons, dynamic wind loading can induce pronounced aerodynamic interference between closely spaced supertall buildings, yet conventional prediction approaches often rely on simplified interference-factor models with limited ability to capture the interactions between adjacent towers and their complex spatiotemporal evolution. This study proposes a dynamic graph learning framework for short-term forecasting of typhoon winds in a twin-tower system. The framework is built upon a spatiotemporal graph neural network (GNN), in which an Adaptive Dynamic Graph Module (ADGM) uses physically meaningful large-scale wind indicators, including the shared background mean wind direction and tower-top mean wind speed, to condition the time-varying inter-tower connectivity. By exploiting these wind-conditioned dynamic graphs, the network maps recent tower-top measurements to future-window along-wind, cross-wind, and vertical turbulence intensities, together with mean wind speeds at both towers. The proposed model is evaluated using rooftop measurements collected from a single pair of closely spaced supertall towers during three typhoon events. Compared with a static-graph model and non-graph GRU/LSTM sequence baselines, the ADGM model improves turbulence-intensity forecasting, yielding relative increases in R2 of up to 27.6% and relative reductions in RMSE of up to 26.4% compared with the static-graph baseline, while providing comparable accuracy for mean-wind statistics. Analysis of the learned edge weights indicates that the learned inter-tower statistical coupling varies systematically with incident wind direction, without prescribing interference patterns a priori. These results demonstrate the feasibility of an aerodynamically conditioned and physically interpretable data-driven approach for site-specific short-term forecasting of coupled rooftop wind characteristics, with potential applications in future monitoring and early-warning systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
