AD  - Anhui Key Laboratory of Civil Engineering Structures and Materials, Hefei University of Technology, Anhui, 230009, China
AD  - Engineering Research Center of Low-Carbon Technology and Equipment for Cement-based Materials, Ministry of Education, Hefei University of Technology, Anhui, 230009, China
AD  - Hefei Royalstar Electronic & Electrical Appliances Group Co., Ltd, Anhui, 231100, China
AB  - Inter-module connections play a crucial role in the seismic resilience of modular steel buildings. However, existing structural systems are often challenged by sensitivity to erection tolerances, complex multi-component details with elevated costs, and access openings that compromise local integrity. To address these challenges, this study introduces a novel envelope-compatible all-bolted connection using U-bolts and plates for low-rise modular steel buildings. The seismic performance of the proposed connection was assessed through an integrated experimental and numerical study. Seven full-scale specimens were tested under quasi-static cyclic loading to evaluate the effects of connection configuration on hysteretic response, strength, stiffness degradation, ductility, and energy dissipation. A complementary finite element (FE) model was developed and validated to support the experimental findings. The results show that the proposed U-bolt-based connection improves deformation compatibility between adjacent columns and provides higher peak resistance than conventional high-strength bolted details at drift ratios exceeding 1.0%. The placement of connectors on the web or flange of the lower column has minimal impact on global strength and stiffness. Bolted restraints at both the upper and lower columns enhance load-path redundancy and deformation capacity, although they introduce asymmetry in the hysteresis loops. This study provides a sound experimental and analytical basis for the design and seismic implementation of the proposed inter-module connection. © 2026
