AB  - In this study, parametric transient state tests using small-scale substructure specimens of multi-storied steel moment-resisting frames at fire are conducted to examine the effects of the load redistribution capacity (redundancy) on the overall collapse temperature after a heated column local-buckled. Three types of specimens with one, two, and three stories and two spanned steel frames are experimentally analysed, and the centre steel column and beams on the first story are heated using an electric furnace. Test results show that the axial force of the heated column is redistributed to the intact side-columns by the lifting resistance force of the beams, and the collapse temperature of the frame specimen increased. A simple calculation model is proposed to evaluate the overall collapse temperature considering the load redistribution capacity, and the calculation results agree with the test results. © 2024 Institution of Structural Engineers
