TI  - Physics-integrated machine-learning framework for predicting torsional strength and response behavior of reinforced concrete beams
PY  - 2026
T2  - Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 181
C7  - 115526
DO  - 10.1016/j.engappai.2026.115526
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043167726?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil Engineering, Chonnam National University, Gwangju, South Korea
AB  - Predicting torsional cracking and failure in reinforced concrete beams remains challenging because of the complex interactions among material properties, geometry, and reinforcement mechanisms. This study proposes a hybrid physics–machine-learning framework that integrates physics-guided neural networks, graph neural networks, and a Bayesian-optimized stacking ensemble to achieve accurate and interpretable torsional strength prediction. A database comprising 300 experimental tests and 500 physics-informed synthetic samples is used for model development and validation. The proposed graph neural network achieves the highest standalone performance (coefficient of determination (R2) = 0.92, root mean squared error (RMSE) = 25 kN-meter (kN·m)), outperforming previously reported machine-learning models and conventional design codes. The Bayesian-optimized stacking ensemble further improves predictive robustness, achieving a test RMSE of approximately 33 kN m. In comparison, the models reported by Yu et al. (2022), including boosted tree ensembles, support vector machines, and two-dimensional convolutional neural networks, yielded mean absolute errors of 119%, 198%, and 184%, respectively. Conventional design codes, including American Concrete Institute 318-25, Canadian Standards Association A23.3-14, and Eurocode 2, exhibit substantially lower predictive accuracies (R2 = 0.55–0.65). Beyond prediction, the framework generates physically consistent failure boundaries across reinforcement domains. Shapley additive explanations analysis confirms interpretability by identifying longitudinal and transverse reinforcement indices as dominant parameters. A graphical user interface is developed for practical engineering application. The dataset and implementation code are publicly available at: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20322665 . Overall, the proposed framework provides an accurate, interpretable, and deployable artificial intelligence tool for structural assessment and torsional design support. © 2026 The Author(s).
