TI  - CGNN-MGS: A Cascaded Heterogeneous Graph Neural Network with Meta-path Ring Structures for Joint Modeling of Contract Clause Text and Structural Dependencies
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3722487
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048782232?origin=resultslist
AD  - Engineering University, Joint Logistics Support Force, PLA, Chongqing, 401331, China
AB  - Contract clauses encode structural dependencies—cross-references, conditionals, overrides—that shape legal interpretation yet are hard to detect from text. Existing automated-review systems focus on clause classification, risk tagging, or retrieval-augmented QA, leaving inter-clause relations largely unmodeled. We recast contract review as link prediction over a heterogeneous graph whose nodes are clauses, parties, defined terms, and obligation items, and propose CGNN-MGS, a Cascaded Graph Neural Network with Multi-level Graph Structures. A legal-domain pretrained encoder initialises text-aware nodes; three structural modules then cascade in increasing semantic depth: adjacency aggregation over typed neighbourhoods, meta-path propagation that preserves intermediate-node signals, and a novel meta-path-ring module that exploits each meta-path's endpoint relation to characterise fine-grained legal patterns such as two clauses jointly constraining one party while cross-referencing each other. Residual gating keeps the text signal intact throughout the cascade. On CUAD-Dep (510 contracts) and our newly annotated ContractDep-ZH (423 contracts, κ = 0.79), CGNN-MGS attains F1 of 0.8136 / 0.7948, surpassing 18 baselines by 3.4 and 4.0 F1 points over the strongest contender; ablations confirm the ring module's contribution, and a case study shows that meta-path attention agrees with expert reasoning in 78.5% of inspected cases. © 2013 IEEE.
