TI  - Effectiveness of roof-level retrofit beams in improving the robustness of existing steel building structures
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Constructional Steel Research
VL  - 246
C7  - 110574
DO  - 10.1016/j.jcsr.2026.110574
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044945125?origin=resultslist
AD  - ICITECH, Universitat Politècnica de València, Camí de Vera s/n, Valencia, 46022, Spain
AB  - Progressive collapse remains a critical concern in steel buildings, particularly in structures designed prior to the implementation of modern robustness-oriented codes. Accidental actions such as explosions, vehicle impacts, or localised deterioration may trigger the sudden loss of load-bearing elements, initiating a disproportionate collapse mechanism. While current design standards increasingly incorporate robustness requirements, many existing buildings were conceived without considering alternative load paths (ALPs) or redundancy under column-removal scenarios. Strengthening such structures poses significant practical challenges, as conventional retrofit solutions often require intrusive local interventions and extensive downtime. In this context, global strengthening strategies with minimal interference that enhance the overall load redistribution capacity of the structure are of particular interest. This study investigates the feasibility and effectiveness of installing high-stiffness retrofit steel beams at roof level to improve progressive collapse resistance by promoting ALPs and modifying global load-transfer mechanisms. Starting from a three-story reference case, five additional buildings were redesigned by systematically varying height, span length, bay arrangement, or slab configuration. For each building, thirteen column-removal scenarios were considered, together with multiple retrofit layouts and cross-sections, resulting in 1014 numerical simulations. Structural performance was evaluated through energy-based pushdown analyses on 3D non-linear finite element models. The results indicate that roof-level retrofit beams can substantially enhance performance by activating ALPs, particularly in multi-storey buildings with few floors (e.g. 3 floors). For taller buildings (e.g. 6–9 floors), retrofit optimisation and column buckling near failure scenarios require special attention. Floor systems and beam-to-column connection typologies also play a pivotal role in load redistribution. © 2026 The Authors.
