TI  - Value Mining of Power Grid Technology Resource Demand by Integrating Causal-Inference-Spatio- Temporal Graph Neural Network
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
VL  - 14
SP  - 80097
EP  - 80113
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3693774
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105039045448?origin=resultslist
AD  - Information Center, Guizhou Power Grid Company Ltd, Guizhou, Guiyang, 550000, China
AB  - The traditional spatio-temporal graph neural networks have challenges such as false correlation interference in the power grid scenarios, insufficient granularity of spatio-temporal dynamic modeling, and lack of causal interpretability. Moreover, the existing methods do not fully integrate the spatio-temporal dynamics of power grid resources and causal inference, resulting in insufficient accuracy in mining the value of power grid technological resource demands. A causal inference - spatio-temporal graph neural network fusion model is constructed. Through a three-level causal perception framework and a four-level value mining framework, it precisely captures the spatio-temporal dynamics of power grid technological resources and mines their demand value, providing support for resource allocation decisions. Experimental verification shows that the F1 value approaches 0.92. The loss on the test set has decreased from 10-4 after 60 iterations to below 10-5. The spatial prediction error of grid resource distribution is only 0.85 km, which is 17.5% to 44.1% lower than that of the comparison model. The relative error of demand value quantification is 6.87%, which is 27.9% to 55.1% lower than that of the comparison model. Moreover, the model has a short response time and strong scene adaptability, effectively improving the accuracy of the dynamic capture of grid scientific and technological resources and demand value mining. It helps to effectively allocate grid resources and optimize technology resource management.  © 2013 IEEE.
