AB  - Urban air mobility (UAM) has emerged as a promising solution for future urban transportation. However, the lack of real-world UAM operational data limits the characterization of actual UAM demand patterns. Therefore, ground taxi OD records are widely used to construct proxy demand representations for early-stage UAM planning. Existing studies often rely on proxy demand scenarios but remain limited in capturing OD structural dependencies and contextual influences under different UAM deployment scenarios. To address this issue, this paper proposes MV-HOD-UAM, a Multi-View Heterogeneous Origin-Destination Proxy Demand Forecasting Model for scenario-based UAM planning. The proposed context-aware multi-view learning framework integrates heterogeneous information into a unified OD representation space and captures proxy demand evolution, functional differences between origins and destinations, and OD-pair interactions through structured modeling. Furthermore, a dynamic directed dependency learning mechanism is developed to characterize asymmetric and time-varying relationships among OD pairs by incorporating historical OD patterns and contextual information. A context-aware temporal attention decoder is also introduced to adaptively identify informative historical time steps under varying scenarios. Experiments on a simulated air taxi dataset demonstrate that MV-HOD-UAM consistently outperforms competitive baselines. Compared with the strongest baseline, MV-HOD-UAM reduces MSE, RMSE, MAE, and MAPE by 27.31%, 15.17%, 15.49%, and 12.57%, respectively. Ablation results further verify the contributions of structured multi-view OD modeling and context-aware temporal attention, demonstrating the effectiveness of structured OD representations for proxy demand forecasting in early-stage UAM planning. © 2026 The Authors.
