AD  - Nantong University, School of Transportation and Civil Engineering, Nantong, 226019, China
AB  - There are many heterogeneous graphs in the real world, including reference, movie, medical, and transportation networks. In the real world, heterogeneous graphs often have multiple types of nodes and different kinds of edges. To learn node representations of heterogeneous graphs, it is necessary to incorporate rich information about target nodes into low-dimensional vector representations for downstream task analysis. However, most existing heterogeneous models only consider information from low-order views and ignore high-order node information. This will cause the target node to aggregate only local structural information while disregarding global structural information. In addition, most existing dual-view fusion methods only use the method of minimizing cosine similarity, ignoring the role of mutual information. They can respectively capture the correlation and similarity between nodes in the graph, so they are equally important. To address these limitations, we propose a low-order and high-order joint learning model (HSGNN) for heterogeneous graph networks. The model is divided into three main parts: a low-order view feature aggregation module, a high-order view feature aggregation module, and a dual-view joint learning module. The low-order view feature aggregation module obtains the low-order information of the target node through layer-by-layer aggregation. The low-level view feature aggregation module obtains local structural information of the target node through layer-by-layer aggregation. The high-order view feature aggregation module utilizes the denoised reachable matrix to obtain the global structural information of the target node. The dual view joint learning module combines cosine similarity and mutual information to learn the correlation and similarity between nodes. Finally, we conducted node classification and node clustering experiments on three real-world datasets, which improved by approximately 2 percent and 10 percent respectively, demonstrating the effectiveness of HSGNN.  © 2013 IEEE.
