TI  - Limited-monitoring damage diagnosis of communication tower clusters via graph kernel similarity and transfer learning
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computer-Aided Civil and Infrastructure Engineering
VL  - 50
C7  - 100193
DO  - 10.1016/j.cacaie.2026.100193
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048174338?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Transportation Science and Engineering, Harbin Institute of Technology, 73 Huanghe Road, Harbin, 150090, China
AB  - To address the engineering challenge of limited monitoring coverage in large-scale communication tower clusters, this paper proposes a physics-guided damage diagnosis framework that integrates graph-kernel structural similarity and transfer learning. The novelty of the proposed method lies not in the independent use of graph kernels or time-series neural networks, but in their coupled use for physically admissible cross-structure response transfer and cluster-level damage diagnosis. First, a time-varying element-attributed graph is constructed to encode tower topology, geometric scale, equivalent top mass, local stiffness degradation, and connection-constraint degradation within a unified mechanical representation. A graph-kernel similarity metric, combined with bidirectional transferable-neighborhood consistency and wind-field coherence screening, is then used to identify high-confidence source-target transfer pairs and reduce the risk of negative transfer. Second, a conditional time-series neural network is developed for tower-top displacement reconstruction. Source-target structural condition vectors and graph-kernel similarity are embedded into a feature modulation module, enabling the measured response of instrumented source towers to be transferred to uninstrumented target towers under a common local wind field. A joint time-frequency physics-guided loss is further introduced to improve dynamic consistency. Finally, a leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) strategy is used to rotate reference towers and construct a multi-source consensus diagnostic index, thereby reducing false alarms caused by environmental anomalies or reference-tower uncertainty. Numerical validation using 9900 source-target transfer cases and field validation on an operating communication tower cluster demonstrate that the proposed framework can reconstruct unmonitored tower responses and identify inspection-confirmed damaged towers under limited monitoring coverage. © 2026 The Author(s).
