TI  - Transferable and adaptable full-field structural response reconstruction from sparse sensors via GraphSAGE–LSTM neural networks
PY  - 2026
T2  - Engineering Structures
VL  - 359
C7  - 122740
DO  - 10.1016/j.engstruct.2026.122740
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035245686?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong
AD  - Joint Research Center for Marine Infrastructure, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong
AD  - School of Engineering and Built Environment, Griffith University, Gold Coast Campus, 4222, QLD, Australia
AD  - Key Laboratory of Concrete & Prestressed Concrete Structures of Ministry of Education, Southeast University, Nanjing, China
AB  - Accurately reconstructing full-field structural responses from sparse sensor measurements remains a fundamental challenge in structural health monitoring. Traditional model-based response reconstruction methods are sensitive to modeling errors, while data-driven approaches require extensive data, lack robustness, and exhibit poor transferability to new structures. This study proposes a novel hybrid framework that integrates a graph sample and aggregate (GraphSAGE) network with a long short-term memory (LSTM) network for effective modeling of spatiotemporally correlated dynamics. In this framework, the GraphSAGE module captures spatial relationships to generate and automatically refine mode shapes, while the LSTM module decouples sparse sensor acceleration data into multi-modal coordinate vectors. These refined mode shapes and modal coordinates enable real-time and accurate reconstruction of the full-field acceleration response, even with extremely sparse sensor deployment. The model is pretrained using finite element simulations from similar structures, embedding intrinsic modal physics to significantly enhance model transferability and adaptability to unseen structures. Furthermore, the framework features online modal calibration, which allows the model to automatically adapt to structural changes without the need for retraining. Validation through three frame case studies demonstrates that the proposed framework reduces response reconstruction error by more than 30% compared with a modal uncorrected baseline, highlighting the superior transferability and adaptability of the developed method. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
