TI  - Intelligent railway component inspection during construction and maintenance via YOLO-GNN architecture
PY  - 2026
T2  - Automation in Construction
VL  - 190
C7  - 107074
DO  - 10.1016/j.autcon.2026.107074
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041325587?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of South Carolina, Columbia, 29208, SC, United States
AB  - The safety and reliability of railway systems depend on the integrity of track components, a challenge exacerbated by the heterogeneity and aging of legacy U.S. infrastructure. Ensuring component quality during construction and maintenance requires precise, context-aware inspection. While deep learning has enabled automated visual inspection, most models cannot differentiate genuinely missing components from intentionally unused installation points, a task demanding structural and spatial reasoning. This paper presents YOLO-GNN (YG-Net), a hybrid framework that integrates YOLO11-seg for rail component detection and segmentation with a graph neural network module, EGATFormer, for spatial-context-aware inference of missing spikes. EGATFormer captures baseplate-level relationships through edge-guided attention masking. To support benchmarking, the Rail Components Dataset (RCD) is introduced, containing 2100 real-world images and over 41,000 annotated components. YG-Net achieves 55.90% mAP, 50.99% Seg-mAP, and 14.76 ms/image inference, while EGATFormer improves missing-spike classification F1-score from 80.59% to 89.21%, approaching the 95.11% upper bound. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
