TI  - Heterophily-adaptive graph neural networks with structural-aware and dynamic residual propagation for intelligent diagnosis
PY  - 2025
T2  - Measurement Science and Technology
VL  - 36
IS  - 8
C7  - 086144
DO  - 10.1088/1361-6501/adfd77
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105014738576?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Intelligent Equipment, Suzhou Vocational Institute of Industrial Technology, Suzhou, 215000, China
AD  - School of Mechanical-Electronic and Vehicle Engineering, Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture, Beijing, 100044, China
AB  - Accurate and efficient fault diagnosis for high-end equipment remains a significant challenge, primarily due to heterogeneous data and complex temporal dependencies in industrial monitoring environments. To address these challenges, a novel structural-aware graph neural network (GNNs) framework is proposed. It transforms multi-source time-series signals into graph-structured data using a hybrid construction strategy that integrates temporal sequencing with similarity-based relationships. This approach enables the model to capture both short-term temporal dependencies and long-range global patterns within complex mechanical systems. The framework incorporates two key mechanisms to enhance diagnostic performance: structural-aware similarity modeling (SASM) and locally adaptive residual feature propagation (LARFP). SASM dynamically adjusts feature aggregation based on node heterogeneity, enhancing feature discriminability in heterogeneous graphs. LARFP mitigates the over-smoothing problem commonly found in deep GNN, ensuring robust feature preservation and effective multi-layer information fusion. Experimental results on three benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed framework consistently outperforms existing methods in classification accuracy, robustness, and computational efficiency. The results confirm the framework’s potential for deployment in intelligent health monitoring and predictive maintenance applications for high-end equipment. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.
