AD  - Anhui Bossco Environmental Protection Technology Co., Ltd, Nanning, 530007, China
AD  - School of Marine Science and Engineering, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, 511442, China
AB  - The steel joint with External Diaphragm (ED) is commonly utilized in practical steel frame systems due to its superior stiffness and strength, aligning with the design requirement of strong joint and weak component. However, the welded connection remains one of the most vulnerable parts within the steel joint. Therefore, this paper proposes a type of Beam Flange Opening (BFO) design tailored for the steel joint with the ED. The effects of the reduced area ratio and location parameter of the BFO on the seismic performance of the steel joint under two different thickness ratios of the ED are investigated through numerical analysis. Subsequently, a preliminary design proposal is summarized. The reliability of the design proposal is verified through a hysteresis test and a series of numerical analyzes considering the fracture of the welded connection. A total of 154 numerical models were established for parametric analysis and reliability verification. Results of the ideal analysis without considering the weld fracture reveal that the hysteresis curve, skeleton curve, and stiffness degeneration curve are more sensitive to the reduced area ratio of the BFO than the location parameter. Moreover, a larger reduced area ratio results in a notable decline in the peak force under the Thickness Ratio (TR) of 1.25. Meanwhile, although the impact of the design parameters of the BFO on ductility is limited, it is noted that a balance between the reduced area ratio and the location parameter is crucial for controlling plastic hinges effectively, therefore a proper combination design for these two parameters is required. Furthermore, the validation results demonstrate that the BFO design achieves higher ductility when considering the weld fracture compared to the design without the BFO, which improves the redundancy and survivability of the steel joint under poor weld quality conditions, and confirms the efficacy of the proposed design parameters of the BFO. Ultimately, the formal design proposal for the steel joint with the ED and BFO is presented. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd
