AB  - Traffic flow forecasting, a critical component of intelligent urban traffic management, faces challenges such as complex spatio-temporal dependencies and diverse road topologies. To overcome the limitations of existing methods that either struggle with graph structure modeling or lack explicit spatio-temporal relationship representation, this paper proposes a novel graph-aware spatio-temporal forecasting model named Modulated Graph Token Network (ModuNet). The model utilizes Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) to extract the topological features of nodes and constructs graph tokens to capture structural information. A modulation transformation matrix, shared across all time steps, is introduced to integrate graph tokens with sequential traffic flow data. Subsequently, a Transformer model with an encoder-decoder architecture is employed to explicitly model four core relationships—node to node, flow to flow, node to flow, and flow to node—within a unified attention mechanism. This design significantly enhances the model’s capacity to capture both short-term and long-term dependencies. Experiments on three public traffic datasets, PeMSD7, PeMSD8 and METR-LA, show that ModuNet consistently outperforms mainstream methods across various prediction horizons, with particularly strong performance in long-term forecasting. The proposed method offers a clear structure, controllable parameters, and strong generalizability, making it well suited for a wide range of graph-based time series modeling tasks. © 2013 IEEE.
