AD  - China Railway 14th Bureau Group Co., Ltd, Jinan, 250101, China
AB  - In the traditional Euler beam model, the longitudinal settlement of prefabricated structures neglects the nonlinear behavior of joints, thereby inadequately capturing the structural deformation behavior. In this study, the nonlinear behavior of joints is defined as discontinuous deformation between components and joints, as well as nonlinear variations in joint rotational stiffness. Firstly, eight models for calculating joint rotational stiffness under axial force and bending moment are introduced. Subsequently, the Timoshenko beam is incorporated to consider the shear and rotational deformation of joints. Furthermore, a novel finite difference solution for the longitudinal deformation under time-varying soil backfill load is derived, employing the Winkler foundation and considering the continuous conditions of bending moments and shear forces between components. The accuracy of the proposed model is confirmed with on-site data. The inflection point is located at the discontinuity of the soil backfilling area, dividing the longitudinal deformation curve into upward convex and downward concave modes. Upon completion of construction, the 56th ring exhibits downward concave, and joint rotation stiffness kθ is 4.36 × 1013N·m rad-1. The longitudinal deformation comprises components and joints, exhibiting spatio-temporal characteristics. Rigid displacement occurs on the components, appearing as linear continuous beams, while discontinuous displacement arises at the joints due to dislocation and rotation. The increased axial force can effectively decrease longitudinal deformation and joint opening, exhibiting a marginal diminishing effect. Considering the safety redundancy requirements, it is recommended that the axial force in prefabricated subway stations exceed 107 N. © 2025 The Author(s).
